      "worked": "The coefficient of $x^2$ is positive, so $f(x)>0$ for every real $x$ exactly when $\\Delta<0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(4k)^2-4(1)(4k+15)&<0\\\\16k^2-16k-60&<0\\\\4k^2-4k-15&<0\\\\(2k+3)(2k-5)&<0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe zeros of $y=(2k+3)(2k-5)$ are $k=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $k=\\frac{5}{2}$. Its upward-opening graph lies below the $k$-axis between these values.\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{3}{2}<k<\\frac{5}{2}$. The endpoints give a repeated root of $f(x)=0$, so they are excluded.",
      "solutionDiagrams": [
